// Plant nutrients as N, P2O5 and K2O (2026-09-26, gardening depth rung 3).
// Read by src/systems/farming/soil.rs, which carries the model and the
// reasoning; this file carries the numbers and where each one came from.
//
// UNITS. Every nutrient amount in the simulation is GRAMS of N, P2O5 and K2O,
// the convention fertilizer labels and extension-service removal tables use,
// so any number can be checked against a bag or a table without converting.
// (For the element: P = 0.436 x P2O5 and K = 0.830 x K2O, from molar masses.)
//
// -- demand_anchor: what a crop takes out of its unit in one season ---------
//
// data/plants.csv carries nutrient_n / nutrient_p / nutrient_k, and they are
// NOT kilograms of anything. Until 2026-09-26 its header called them
// "kg/hectare equivalent, scaled to single plant", but tomato's 0.15 / 0.05 /
// 0.20 is no real per-plant or per-hectare figure; schemas/plant.toml and the
// Library describe them as RELATIVE demand (higher means hungrier), and that
// is how they are used here (the header now says so).
//
// So one crop with a published removal figure fixes the scale, and every
// other crop is read against it. A crop's season need, per nutrient:
//
//     need (g) = expected harvest (kg) x anchor removal (g per kg)
//                x (crop's plants.csv index / anchor's plants.csv index)
//
// The expected harvest is the middle of the crop's plants.csv yield range times
// the items.csv mass of what it harvests into, so a crop that yields more
// takes more out, and the index sets how rich that harvest is in each
// nutrient relative to a tomato. For the tomato itself: yield 2 to 8 fruit of
// 0.2 kg = 1.0 kg, so its season need is exactly the removal below.
//
// Removal, not whole-plant uptake: the soil loses what LEAVES in the harvest.
// Stems, leaves and roots are assumed to stay in the unit or go back through
// the composter (CDFA below: the fruit is about two thirds of the crop's
// above-ground N). This is the basis the Wisconsin guide uses for maintaining
// P and K.
//
// Tomato, fresh fruit. 1 lb per short ton (2,000 lb) = 0.5 g per kg.
//   N:    3 lb N/ton = 1.5 g/kg. California Department of Food and Agriculture,
//         FREP California Fertilization Guidelines, "Processing Tomatoes"
//         (https://www.cdfa.ca.gov/is/ffldrs/frep/FertilizationGuidelines/Tomato.html,
//         read 2026-09-26): "the fruits contained on average 3 lbs N/ton, which
//         accounted for about two thirds of the total N in the aboveground biomass".
//   P2O5: 1.8 lb/ton = 0.9 g/kg. University of Wisconsin-Madison Extension,
//         "Nutrient application guidelines for field, vegetable, and fruit crops
//         in Wisconsin" (A2809), Table 4.2, row "Tomato": crop removal 1.8 lb P2O5
//         and 8 lb K2O per ton fresh
//         (https://cropsandsoils.extension.wisc.edu/files/2024/07/Table-4.2.pdf,
//         read 2026-09-26).
//   K2O:  8 lb/ton = 4.0 g/kg. Same table and row.
//   Range, for the record: Oregon State EM 9165 (Sullivan et al. 2017) Table 3
//   has a 12 ton/acre tomato removing 10 lb P2O5 and 80 lb K2O, which is 0.83
//   and 6.7 lb/ton; the CDFA page gives 4 to 6 lb K/ton (4.8 to 7.2 lb K2O).
//   The Wisconsin figures sit at the top of those ranges and are one table.
//
// WHAT THIS GETS WRONG, stated so nobody trusts it further than it goes: the
// plants.csv indices do not track real removal between crop kinds. Read this
// way, a wheat unit (7 kg of grain) needs 7.0 g N, 5.0 g P2O5 and 8.4 g K2O a
// season, where Wisconsin A2809 puts wheat grain at 0.5 lb P2O5 and 0.35 lb
// K2O per 60 lb bushel (58 g and 41 g for 7 kg) and grain protein puts its N
// near 20 g/kg (about 140 g). Grain and dry legumes are dense in nutrients and
// the indices were not built from that. FIXED 2026-09-26 for the crops that
// matter most: plants.csv now carries each crop's own removal in g per kg of
// harvest (REMOVAL COLUMNS, below), and a crop with those columns does not use
// this anchor at all. The index reading above is now only the fallback for a
// crop whose columns are still blank, one nutrient at a time.
//
// -- REMOVAL COLUMNS: plants.csv removal_n_g_per_kg, removal_p2o5_g_per_kg,
//    removal_k2o_g_per_kg (2026-09-26) ---------------------------------------
//
// A crop whose removal column is filled needs, of that nutrient, exactly
//
//     need (g) = removal (g per kg) x expected harvest (kg)
//
// with the expected harvest as above (mid yield x the items.csv item mass).
// The grams are per kg of the harvest AS THE GAME'S ITEM REPRESENTS IT: fresh
// produce as picked, grain and dry seed at the storage moisture its source
// table reports, and for the pulses the dry seed, because data/food/
// crop_nutrition.ron counts bean, soybean, lentil and chickpea as dry seed (the
// pea it counts as green peas, so the pea row is green peas). Legumes still fix
// their n_fixed_pct share of this N from the air.
//
// SOURCES, all read 2026-09-26:
//   A2809  University of Wisconsin-Madison Extension, "Nutrient application
//          guidelines for field, vegetable, and fruit crops in Wisconsin" (A2809),
//          Table 4.2, crop removal of P2O5 and K2O in lb per unit of yield at the
//          table's reporting moisture (the same table as the anchor, URL above).
//          lb per ton x 0.5, lb per cwt x 10, lb per lb x 1000, and lb per bushel
//          / the bushel's weight in lb x 1000, give g per kg.
//   NRCS   USDA Soil Conservation Service, Agricultural Waste Management Field
//          Handbook, Part 651, Chapter 6 "Role of Plants in Waste Management"
//          (210-AWMFH, 4/92; the table pages rev. 1, July 1996), Table 6-6 "Plant
//          nutrient uptake by specified crop and removed in the harvested part of
//          the crop" (from Kilmer 1982; Morrison 1956; Sanchez 1976; USDA 1985):
//          percent N, P and K of the harvested part (https://efotg.sc.egov.usda.gov/
//          references/Delete/2016-8-13/AWMFH_Chapter_6_Role_of_Plants_in_Waste_Man.pdf).
//          Its "Dry wt. lb/bu" column gives the bushel weights used here: wheat
//          and soybeans 60, barley 48, oats 32, rye and sorghum 56. Its own worked
//          example applies a grain percent to the bushel weight ("corn grain: 130
//          bu/ac @ 56 lb/bu = 7,280 lb" and "1.61% N x 7,280 lb = 117 lb N"), so a
//          grain percent is read as percent of the grain as harvested. P x 2.29 =
//          P2O5 and K x 1.2 = K2O, the factors used for urine below. (The 2012
//          revision of the chapter dropped N, P and K from Table 6-6 in favour of
//          the USDA PLANTS Crop Nutrient Tool, which no longer loads as a page.)
//   FDC    USDA FoodData Central, SR Legacy, April 2018 release (the file
//          FoodData_Central_sr_legacy_food_csv_2018-04.zip from fdc.nal.usda.gov):
//          protein, phosphorus and potassium per 100 g of the raw food, cited by FDC
//          id. N = protein / the food's own nitrogen-to-protein factor in that
//          release (6.25 for every food used here), which undoes how USDA got the
//          protein from the nitrogen it measured. P2O5 = P x 2.29, K2O = K x 1.2.
//   CDFA   the tomato's N, as in demand_anchor above.
//
// THE RULE, and why. Grain, dry pulses and oilseeds: N from NRCS, P2O5 and K2O
// from A2809, each falling back to the next source when the crop is missing.
// Fresh produce: N is the N USDA measured in the food (FDC), P2O5 and K2O from
// A2809 where it lists the crop, else FDC. Why FDC and not NRCS for fresh N:
// NRCS's fresh vegetable rows run up to three times the measured N of the same
// produce (tomato 0.30% N, against FDC's 0.14% and CDFA's measured 3 lb per
// ton, 0.15%; bell pepper 0.40% against 0.14%, and that row's P and K are two
// to five times A2809's too), while for the one fresh crop with a measured
// removal (CDFA's tomato, 1.5 g per kg) FDC agrees within 6% (1.41). For the
// dry harvests NRCS and FDC agree within about 20% (wheat 2.08% N against
// 2.16%, soybean 6.25% against 6.39%, dry bean 3.13% against 3.57 to 3.77%;
// oats aside, where FDC weighs groats), so the removal table is used there.
//
// WHERE THE SOURCES DISAGREE, stated so each choice can be checked:
//   - FDC-based P2O5 and K2O run below A2809 for the same crop: A2809's lettuce
//     K2O (4.55 g/kg) is twice FDC's (2.33), its onion P2O5 1.8 times. So the
//     rows that had to use FDC (kale, turnip, radish, leek, garlic, the herbs
//     and others marked FDC below) may understate P and K by up to half.
//   - A2809's per-pound rows run two to three times the P and K USDA measured
//     in the same produce: pea, canning (0.0046 lb P2O5 and 0.0092 lb K2O per
//     lb, against FDC green peas' 2.47 and 2.93 g/kg) and sunflower K2O (0.024
//     lb per lb would be 2.0% K in the seed, against 0.65% in FDC's kernels).
//     For the pea FDC is used, for sunflower K2O NRCS (1.11% K). Asparagus,
//     buckwheat and triticale, which would need those rows too, are left blank
//     rather than guessed.
//   - oat: the item says groats, the tables weigh whole oats with the hull. Per
//     kg of groats the removal would be about a quarter higher.
// Blank on purpose: flax (its harvest item is a fiber bundle, not seed or straw,
// and no table weighs that) and the mushrooms (they feed on their substrate,
// straw or sawdust with bran, which is replaced with each block rather than
// fertilized; filling their removal would bill the garden's compost and urine
// for nitrogen the substrate supplies). Every other blank is simply not yet
// sourced.
//
// Per crop (64): N / P2O5 / K2O in g per kg of harvest, its basis, then where
// each number came from (FDC ids are SR Legacy; "x / 6.25" is its protein over
// its nitrogen factor):
//   wheat 20.8 / 8.33 / 5.83: grain at 13.5% moisture. N: NRCS wheat 2.08%. P2O5: A2809
//       wheat grain 0.5 lb/bu of 60 lb. K2O: A2809 0.35 lb/bu.
//   barley 18.2 / 8.33 / 7.29: grain at 14.5% moisture. N: NRCS barley 1.82%. P2O5: A2809
//       barley grain 0.4 lb/bu of 48 lb. K2O: A2809 0.35 lb/bu.
//   oat 19.5 / 9.06 / 5.94: whole grain at 14% moisture (the item says groats). N: NRCS
//       oats 1.95%. P2O5: A2809 oat grain 0.29 lb/bu of 32 lb. K2O: A2809 0.19 lb/bu.
//   rye 20.8 / 7.32 / 5.54: grain at 14% moisture. N: NRCS rye 2.08%. P2O5: A2809 rye grain
//       0.41 lb/bu of 56 lb. K2O: A2809 0.31 lb/bu.
//   sorghum 16.7 / 7.14 / 7.14: grain at 14% moisture. N: NRCS sorghum 1.67%. P2O5: A2809
//       sorghum grain 0.4 lb/bu of 56 lb. K2O: A2809 0.4 lb/bu.
//   rice 13.9 / 5.5 / 2.76: paddy (rough) rice. N: NRCS rice 1.39%. P2O5: NRCS 0.24% P
//       (A2809 has no rice). K2O: NRCS 0.23% K.
//   bean 31.3 / 12 / 16: dry seed at 18% moisture (crop_nutrition.ron: dry). N: NRCS beans,
//       dry 3.13%. P2O5: A2809 bean, dry (kidney, navy) 1.2 lb/cwt. K2O: A2809 1.6 lb/cwt.
//   kidney_bean 31.3 / 12 / 16: dry seed at 18% moisture (crop_nutrition.ron: dry). N: NRCS
//       beans, dry 3.13%. P2O5: A2809 bean, dry (kidney, navy) 1.2 lb/cwt. K2O: A2809 1.6
//       lb/cwt.
//   pinto_bean 31.3 / 12 / 16: dry seed at 18% moisture (crop_nutrition.ron: dry). N: NRCS
//       beans, dry 3.13%. P2O5: A2809 bean, dry (kidney, navy) 1.2 lb/cwt. K2O: A2809 1.6
//       lb/cwt.
//   navy_bean 31.3 / 12 / 16: dry seed at 18% moisture (crop_nutrition.ron: dry). N: NRCS
//       beans, dry 3.13%. P2O5: A2809 bean, dry (kidney, navy) 1.2 lb/cwt. K2O: A2809 1.6
//       lb/cwt.
//   soybean 62.5 / 13.3 / 23.3: grain at 13% moisture. N: NRCS soybeans 6.25%. P2O5: A2809
//       soybean grain 0.8 lb/bu of 60 lb. K2O: A2809 1.4 lb/bu.
//   chickpea 32.8 / 10 / 12: dry seed at 10% moisture. N: FDC 173756 chickpeas, mature
//       seeds, raw: protein 20.47 / 6.25. P2O5: A2809 pea, chick/field/cow 20 lb/ton. K2O:
